course: english
lesson: 48
title: Spoken language: presenting and show-and-tell
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Giving a talk is a skill, like riding a bike: wobbly at first, smoother with every practice.
explain: The audience matters most: speak so the back row can hear, look up at them, and choose words that suit them, chatty for classmates, formal for the school governors (the grown-ups who help run the school).
explain: A good talk has a clear start (say what it is about, with a hook to grab attention), a middle (a few key points) and an end (sum up, then take questions).
explain: Cue cards with key words beat reading every word from a sheet, and slides should hold pictures and a few key words while you say the rest.
explain: Picture Kai in Liverpool before his talk: "I'm proper nervous, our kid" ("proper" means really; "our kid" means my brother or sister). Nerves are normal, even for adults, and practice, a slow breath and a smile help most.
fact: Show-and-tell builds confidence in speaking to a group; the object does not need to be exciting, because the talk matters more.
fact: A good show-and-tell says what the object is, why it is special and one interesting fact about it.
fact: Speaking loudly and clearly enough for the back row matters more than fancy words; this is called projecting your voice.
fact: Eye contact helps the audience listen, while reading every word with your head down sounds flat.
fact: Cue cards hold key words only, so you can look up often.
fact: Visual aids such as slides, pictures or objects help the audience follow, but you should not read the slides out word for word.
fact: Register means choosing words to suit your audience: formal language for governors, chattier words for friends.
fact: Filler words such as "like", "um" and "erm" are natural, but too many distract listeners; a short pause works better.
fact: Confident speakers still feel nervous; practising, breathing slowly and starting with a smile help.
fact: When you cannot answer a question after a talk, it is fine to say "Good question, I'll find out" instead of making something up.
fact: Useful feedback says what was good and why, for example "Your facts about lava were really clear"; "That presentation ate" is Gen Z slang for "it was excellent".
fact: "G'day" is a friendly Australian greeting, fine in a relaxed class talk; dialect greetings are not wrong, they just suit some audiences better than others.
fact: In Scotland "wee" means small, so "wee man" is a fond way to say "little lad".
exercise: Ruby brings a smooth beach stone to show-and-tell and some children giggle. What matters most? | How well she talks about it | How rare and valuable the stone is | How big and heavy the stone is | How much the stone cost to buy
exercise: Which three things make a good show-and-tell? | What it is, why it's special, one fact | Its price, its weight and its colour | A joke, a song and a long story | Where you got it and who else has one
exercise: Leo reads every word from his paper, head down. What should he do? | Use key-word cards and look up often | Read faster so the talk ends sooner | Hold the paper up higher, over his face | Learn it all by heart and say it at top speed
exercise: The teacher says, "Speak so the back row can hear." What is this called? | Projecting your voice | Shouting at the audience | Whispering clearly | Using long words
exercise: What should go on good cue cards? | Key words only | Every word of the talk, in full | Jokes to read out loud | Nothing; they stay blank
exercise: Year 6 uses slides for a talk on volcanoes. What should the slides show? | Pictures and a few key words | Every sentence of the talk | Lots of small writing to read | Only the title, in giant letters
exercise: Kai says, "I'm proper nervous, our kid." What does "our kid" mean? | My brother or sister | My own little child | My best mate at school | My teacher
exercise: Is it normal to feel nervous before a talk? | Yes, even adults feel it | No, only shy people feel it | No, not if you are clever | Yes, but only young children
exercise: Which helps most with nerves before a talk? | Practise, breathe slowly, smile | Skip breakfast and rush in late | Stay up all night learning it | Tell the class you will mess up
exercise: A mum in Glasgow says, "Wee man, ye say 'like' every two words." What is she pointing out? | Too many filler words | A simile used wrongly | Speaking far too slowly | A talk that is too short
exercise: What can you do instead of saying "um"? | Pause for a moment | Say "like" instead | Speak much faster | Cough loudly
exercise: After a talk Ella says, "That presentation ate." What does she mean? | It was excellent | It was too long | It was boring and slow | It was scary
exercise: Which feedback is the most useful? | "Your facts about lava were really clear." | "Good." | "That was okay, I suppose, whatever." | "I liked it, I think, it was a talk."
exercise: Your audience is the school governors. Which opening fits? | Good morning, I'd like to tell you about our garden. | Alright lads, so yeah, the garden thing, innit. | Yo, listen up, it's garden time, let's go. | Hiya, so like, um, gardens are good and that.
exercise: A child in Sydney starts, "G'day, I'm talkin' about koalas." Is that okay? | Yes, for a relaxed class talk | No, dialect greetings are always wrong | No, every talk must start with a joke | Yes, and in a formal speech above all
exercise: Someone asks a question you cannot answer. What should you say? | "Good question, I'll find out." | Make up a clever-sounding answer | "That's a stupid question. Next." | Pretend you did not hear it at all
test: Jake says, "Show-and-tell's pointless if your thing's rubbish." Put him right. | The talk matters more than the object | Only exciting objects count at all | Show-and-tell is only for Year 6 | Objects have to be bought, not found
test: What would happen if Leo kept his head down and read every word? | His talk would sound flat and lose the audience | Everyone would listen harder, as it is correct | It would be shorter and therefore much better | The audience would hear him more clearly
test: Bradley, Gen Alpha: "My talk's sigma, no cap, I don't need practice." ("No cap" means no lie; "sigma" is slang for cool and needing nobody.) What is true? | Even confident speakers improve with practice | Practice is only for nervous people | Confident people never need to rehearse | Practising makes a talk sound worse
test: A cartoon presenter turns his back and reads ten slides aloud, and everyone cheers. What would really happen? | People would drift off; he should face them | People would love it, as the slides are the talk | Nobody would notice which way he faced | The audience would beg for more text
test: Nan from Newcastle: "Howay, pet, speak up, I cannit hear ye." ("Howay" means come on; "cannit" means can't.) What should you do? | Speak louder and clearer, to the back | Speak faster to get to the end | Whisper so you don't seem rude | Stop the talk and sit down
test: Which talk ending is best? | "So volcanoes build land and destroy it. Any questions?" | "Um, that's it, I think, yeah, I'm done now, bye." | "I forgot the rest of it, so that is the end now, bye then." | "The end. And please don't ask me anything."
test: Dev, who speaks Indian English, says, "Kindly prepone my talk to Monday." ("Prepone" means move earlier.) When does Dev want to speak? | Earlier, on Monday | Later, after Monday | Never; he is cancelling | At the same time as planned
test: Your mate snaps, "Shut up, I'm not nervous, I'm just shaking for fun." What is going on? | He is nervous but hiding it | He is truly calm and fine | He is cold and needs a jumper | He is angry with the teacher
test: Which is the best order for a talk? | Hook and topic, key points, sum up, questions | Questions first, then the points, then a hook | Key points, then the hook, then stop dead | Sum up first, then the hook, then the topic
test: Leah gives a talk to her mates, then the same talk to the governors. What should change? | Her words: chatty for mates, formal for governors | Nothing; one talk fits every audience | Only the font size on her slides | The facts, which can be made up for governors
test: Sam's talk lasts 2 minutes, but he says "um" 30 times. Which change helps the audience most? | Pausing instead of saying "um" | Talking faster to fit in more | Adding slides packed with text | Using longer, posher words
exam: Identify the practice whereby a speaker adjusts vocabulary and formality to suit a particular audience. | Register | Projection | Alliteration | Rebuttal
exam: Select the response most consistent with recommended practice in respect of the employment of cue cards. | They should bear key words only | They should bear the full text verbatim | They should be read with the head lowered | They should be concealed and never consulted
exam: It is given that a presenter is unable to answer a question posed by the audience. Identify the most appropriate course of action. | Acknowledge it and undertake to find out | Invent a plausible answer forthwith and proceed | Decline to acknowledge the question | Terminate the presentation at once
exam: Ascertain which of the following most accurately characterises nervousness prior to a presentation. | It is normal, even among adults | It indicates an absence of ability | It afflicts only the unprepared | It is confined to young children
